<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#calligraphy">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calligraphy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Calligraphy is the art of decorative handwriting, highly developed in the Islamic world, where it adorned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, buildings and objects. Persian calligraphy, especially the nastaliq script, was a prestigious art at Iranian courts. It formed part of the cultural patronage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calligraphy.jpg" alt="Calligraphy" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Letter_by_Ouyang_Xiu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Calligraphy, from the Greek kalligraphía, meaning beautiful writing, is fine handwriting as a visual art. It involves the design and execution of lettering or characters with a pen, an ink brush or another writing instrument, and it differs from type design. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and the Islamic world it is a prominent element of art, and its visual form is often shaped by the meaning of the text. Tools vary by tradition: quills, dip pens and reed pens in the West and the Islamic world, and in East Asia the Four Treasures of the Study, the ink brush, inkstick, paper and inkstone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the oldest known characters are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oracle-bone-script">oracle bone script</a> carved on ox scapulae and tortoise plastrons under the Shang dynasty. Around 220 BCE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> imposed reforms that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a>'s unification of 3300 standardised small seal characters. The regular script was largely finalised by Zhong You (151–230), and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang</a> (926–933) ordered the classics printed in it from wooden blocks.</p><p>Islamic calligraphy evolved alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a> and is associated with the geometric art of mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. It reached special prominence in the Ottoman period. Nasta'liq, which Persian calligraphers call the bride of calligraphy scripts, is the most popular classical Persian style and was often used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> under Mughal rule. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, monasteries were the main preservers of the Roman letter forms after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> recruited, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a>'s words, a crowd of scribes, and Alcuin developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule">Carolingian minuscule</a>, from which modern book type descends; its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, the Godescalc Evangelistary, was finished in 783. In the eleventh century it evolved into blackletter, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg">Johannes Gutenberg</a> adopted as the first European typeface in 1454. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing</a> led illuminated manuscripts to decline, but the modern revival of calligraphy began at the end of the 19th century under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-morris">William Morris</a> and the Arts and Crafts movement. Edward Johnston, whose handbook Writing &amp; Illuminating, &amp; Lettering appeared in 1906, is regarded as the father of modern calligraphy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Calligraphy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…to the poems composed at the Orchid Pavilion gathering of 353 became the most celebrated work of Chinese <b>calligraphy</b>, was revered as the 'sage of calligraphy'; the painter Gu Kaizhi, to whom the Admonitions of the Instructress to the Court Ladies scroll is attributed, was among the first…</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the arts of the book Pir Budaq, governor of Fars and Baghdad, sponsored innovations in nasta'liq <b>calligraphy</b> and finely illustrated manuscripts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Shiraz remained an active centre of poetry, painting and <b>calligraphy</b>, and Karim Khan restored the tomb of Hafez in 1773, while the oil paintings and lacquerwork of the Zand court foreshadowed the Qajar court style that followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The scholar-officials, the literati, were the governing class of China, and their education in the classics, history, poetry and <b>calligraphy</b> defined elite culture.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>It deeply influenced Chinese poetry, from Tao Yuanming to Li Bai, landscape painting, with its vast mountains and tiny human figures, and <b>calligraphy</b>; the great calligrapher Wang Xizhi came from a family of Celestial Master followers.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The study of the Quran and its language gave rise to Arabic grammar, lexicography and <b>calligraphy</b>, which became the most honoured art of the Islamic world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
